{{also|Regent|régent}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₃reǵ-}}
From {{der|en|xno|regent}}, {{der|en|frm|regent}}, and their source, {{der|en|la|regēns|t=ruling; ruler, governor, prince}}, present participle of {{m|la|regō|t=I govern, I steer}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹiːdʒənt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-regent.wav|Audio (RP)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|now|_|rare}} A [[ruler]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
# One who [[rule]]s in place of the [[monarch]], especially because the monarch is too [[young]], [[absent]], or [[disable]]d. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
# {{lb|en|now|_|chiefly|_|historical}} A member of a [[municipal]] or [[civic]] body of [[governors]], especially in certain European cities. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* '''1999''', {{w|Philipp Blom}}, translating Geert Mak, ''Amsterdam: A Brief Life of the City'', Vintage 2001, p. 139:
#*: This perception, however, does no justice to the '''regents''' of the city of Amsterdam.
# {{lb|en|Scotland|North America}} A member of governing board of a [[college]] or [[university]]; also a governor of the Smithsonian Institute in Washington DC. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
# {{lb|en|Indonesia}} The chief executive of a [[regency]]

